1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:13,903
This video shows the web player in plain JavaScript, with no framework. One call puts the animation on the page, and a few more control it. They are the same calls the API ref property gives you in React.

2
00:00:14,433 --> 00:00:22,035
Our ball is already in the public folder, as ball.json, and the page has an empty scene. Let's create a player that fills it.

3
00:00:30,867 --> 00:00:40,506
Create animator takes the address of the file and the element to fill. With nothing else, the file decides, so it starts on load and repeats forever.

4
00:00:41,733 --> 00:00:46,279
The page also has a slider. Let's make it move the player to any moment.

5
00:00:58,967 --> 00:01:05,863
On every move, the player pauses, and set current progress moves it to that point, from zero to one.

6
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:08,014
Let's drag it.

7
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,482
The ball follows the slider exactly.

8
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:22,395
We can also jump to an exact time, and play from there. Let's wire up those two buttons.

9
00:01:34,733 --> 00:01:42,126
Set current time takes milliseconds, so five hundred is the bottom of the bounce. Play starts it from wherever it is.

10
00:01:43,367 --> 00:01:47,102
Let's stop it with the slider, jump to the bottom, and play from there.

11
00:01:51,933 --> 00:01:56,426
The jump lands on the bottom of the bounce, and play carries on from there.

12
00:01:57,667 --> 00:02:01,402
Speed is one more call. Let's wire up the speed buttons.

13
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:19,337
Each speed button calls set playback rate with its own number, half or double.

14
00:02:20,567 --> 00:02:23,701
Let's try double speed, and then half speed.

15
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:34,143
Twice as fast, then half as fast. The player keeps its place, and only the speed changes.

16
00:02:35,367 --> 00:02:47,070
So, without any framework, one call creates the player, and a few more control it. Progress, time, play and speed work the same in every Pixodesk player.
